STEP 10 — Database schema with migrations (Alembic)

What I did:
- Installed Alembic and SQLAlchemy in backend/venv and updated
  backend/requirements.txt (added: alembic, SQLAlchemy, Mako, MarkupSafe).
- Created the 16 tables of the "inspektorati" database with ONE migration:
    backend/migrations/versions/0001_init_schema.py
  All tables are InnoDB, utf8mb4 / utf8mb4_unicode_ci. Every foreign key has
  its own index and the ON DELETE rule from the spec. No data was seeded.

Why Alembic (and not hand-written .sql files):
- It is the standard migration tool for Python + SQLAlchemy and keeps a
  version history of the database schema (like git, but for tables).
- It records which migrations already ran in the table "alembic_version",
  so "upgrade" only runs what is new.
- Every migration has an upgrade() and a downgrade(), so changes can be
  rolled back.
- The SQLAlchemy models (app/db/models.py) describe the same schema in
  Python. "alembic check" compares them to the real database and tells us
  if they ever drift apart. Later features can use the models to read and
  write data.

Files added:
- backend/alembic.ini
    Alembic settings. It has NO database URL inside; the URL is built
    from backend/.env so the password lives in one place only.
- backend/migrations/env.py
    Connects Alembic to MySQL using app.core.config (the .env values)
    and points it at the models.
- backend/migrations/versions/0001_init_schema.py
    The migration itself: creates the 16 tables in dependency order
    (upgrade) and drops them in reverse order (downgrade).
- backend/app/db/models.py
    One SQLAlchemy class per table.

Migration commands (run from inside backend/):
    venv\Scripts\python.exe -m alembic upgrade head      -> create / update all tables
    venv\Scripts\python.exe -m alembic downgrade -1      -> undo the last migration
    venv\Scripts\python.exe -m alembic downgrade base    -> undo ALL migrations (drops the 16 tables and their data)
    venv\Scripts\python.exe -m alembic current           -> show which migration the DB is at
    venv\Scripts\python.exe -m alembic history           -> list all migrations
    venv\Scripts\python.exe -m alembic check             -> confirm models and DB match

Adding a new migration later (after changing app/db/models.py):
    venv\Scripts\python.exe -m alembic revision --autogenerate -m "short description"
    (always read the generated file before running "upgrade head")

